      "id": 1,
      "text": "Waterville Valley became a major tourist destination in 1965 when Tom Corcoran, a two‑time Olympic skier, opened the ski resort.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.waterville.com/about-town-of-waterville-valley",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The resort has hosted 11 World Cup races, drawing stars like Alberto Tomba and Ingemar Stenmark to its slopes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.waterville.com/about-town-of-waterville-valley",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name changed to Waterville Valley in 1967, reflecting its growing identity as a mountain retreat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterville_Valley%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Mad River winds through the town, offering scenic views from spring to winter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
